<% content_for :title, "Stimulus" %>
<% content_for :breadcrumb do %>
  <%= poetry_breadcrumb do |crumb| %>
    <% crumb.with_item("Docs", href: introduction_path) %>
    <% crumb.with_item("Stimulus") %>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
<% snippets = stimulus_guide_snippets %>

<div class="flex flex-col gap-8">
  <header class="flex flex-col gap-3">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Stimulus</h1>
    <p class="max-w-2xl text-lg text-muted-foreground">
      Poetry's behavior is Stimulus, so yours composes with it: attach a
      controller to any component, react to what a component does, drive
      it from your markup, declare the wiring in Ruby, or extend a
      controller in JavaScript. Every rung is checked.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <%= poetry_typeset(preset: "docs", class: "max-w-2xl") do %>
      <h2 id="model">The model</h2>
      <p>
        Each component declares its Stimulus wiring in Ruby through
        <code>use_stimulus</code>, per element: which controllers register
        on the root, which targets, values, and actions each part carries.
        The rendered attributes are that declaration, validated against
        the controllers manifest before the first render. You never write
        a Poetry identifier to make a component work; you write your own
        controllers next to Poetry's, and the two never clobber each other.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <%= poetry_typeset(preset: "docs", class: "max-w-2xl") do %>
      <h2 id="compose">1. Attach your controller to a component</h2>
      <p>
        Every helper and every slot setter accepts <code>data:</code> (with
        <code>class:</code>, <code>id:</code>, and <code>aria:</code>), and the
        root merges it through Poetry's attribute plumbing: Stimulus keys
        concatenate instead of replacing, classes merge, nested hashes
        flatten. Your controller lands on the same element as Poetry's,
        your values ride along, your action sits next to theirs.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= poetry_code_block(code: snippets[:compose], language: "erb", label: "Compose") %>
    <%= poetry_code_block(code: snippets[:compose_output], language: "html", label: "Renders") %>
    <%= poetry_typeset(preset: "docs", class: "max-w-2xl") do %>
      <p>
        A wrapper works too: a <code>&lt;div data-controller="cart"&gt;</code>
        around the component puts everything inside it in your controller's
        scope, which is plain Stimulus.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <%= poetry_typeset(preset: "docs", class: "max-w-2xl") do %>
      <h2 id="listen">2. React to what a component does</h2>
      <p>
        Forty-two of the fifty-two controllers dispatch namespaced events
        (<code>poetry:tabs:change</code>, <code>poetry:combobox:select</code>,
        <code>poetry:calendar:change</code>, <code>poetry:clipboard-text:copied</code>,
        and so on; each component page lists its own). Listen on the root
        or on any ancestor. Overlay content that portals to
        <code>&lt;body&gt;</code> while open still reaches listeners at the
        original position, because the portal bridge re-dispatches every
        declared event there.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= poetry_code_block(code: snippets[:listen], language: "erb", label: "Listen") %>
    <%= poetry_typeset(preset: "docs", class: "max-w-2xl") do %>
      <p>
        Dialog is one of the few that dispatch nothing of their own: its
        state is the <code>data-open</code> attribute, and the native
        <code>&lt;dialog&gt;</code> <code>cancel</code> and <code>close</code>
        events fire as usual.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <%= poetry_typeset(preset: "docs", class: "max-w-2xl") do %>
      <h2 id="drive">3. Drive a component from your markup</h2>
      <p>
        Any element inside a component's scope can call the component's
        actions, the same way the component's own parts do. A Cancel
        button inside a dialog closes it with one descriptor; there is no
        JavaScript to write. From outside the scope, use the trigger slot,
        or the <%= link_to "WebMCP", webmcp_path %> tool surface for agents.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= poetry_code_block(code: snippets[:drive], language: "erb", label: "Drive") %>
    <%= poetry_typeset(preset: "docs", class: "max-w-2xl") do %>
      <p>
        The identifier, the action, the targets, and the typed values you
        write are all validated by <code>poetry:check</code>, whether they
        sit in an attribute or in a helper's <code>data:</code> keywords,
        with a did-you-mean when a name is off by a letter.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <%= poetry_typeset(preset: "docs", class: "max-w-2xl") do %>
      <h2 id="declare">4. Declare it in Ruby</h2>
      <p>
        When the wiring is part of a component's contract rather than
        something to repeat at every call site, subclass the component and
        declare it with the same DSL Poetry uses. Symbols resolve against
        Poetry's manifest and are validated at class load; a String is your
        own identifier and passes through verbatim. <code>extend: true</code>
        appends to the inherited element; redeclaring an element without it
        replaces the element wholesale, which is how Sheet and Drawer put
        their own controllers on Dialog's root.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= poetry_code_block(code: snippets[:declare], language: "ruby", label: "Subclass") %>
    <%= poetry_typeset(preset: "docs", class: "max-w-2xl") do %>
      <p>
        For wiring too dynamic to declare, <code>stimulus_attributes</code>
        hands you one builder per controller, all sharing a single
        attributes instance, so multi-controller merges are correct by
        construction.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= poetry_code_block(code: snippets[:escape_hatch], language: "ruby", label: "Escape hatch") %>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <%= poetry_typeset(preset: "docs", class: "max-w-2xl") do %>
      <h2 id="extend">5. Change what a Poetry controller does</h2>
      <p>
        Every Poetry controller is importable by its specifier, so a
        subclass can override or wrap its methods. Register the subclass
        under the same identifier after Poetry's registration and Stimulus
        unloads the earlier definition: your class takes over everywhere
        that identifier appears, and the registration guard still sees a
        registered controller.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= poetry_code_block(code: snippets[:extend_js], language: "javascript", label: "Extend a controller") %>
    <%= poetry_typeset(preset: "docs", class: "max-w-2xl") do %>
      <p>
        Two things to know before you reach for this rung. The manifest
        still describes the base controller, so a new action name on the
        same identifier reads as unknown to <code>poetry:check</code>; put
        new behavior in a controller of your own and compose it instead.
        And you own the drift: an upgrade that changes the base's
        internals changes your subclass's ground. Rungs one to four express
        nearly everything; this one is for the rest.
      </p>
    <% end %>
  </section>

  <section class="flex flex-col gap-4">
    <%= poetry_typeset(preset: "docs", class: "max-w-2xl") do %>
      <h2 id="tooling">Checked at every rung</h2>
      <p>
        <code>poetry:check</code> validates every reference to a Poetry
        controller statically. Stimulus itself never errors on an
        identifier nothing registered (the element just stays inert), and
        one failed import in your controllers graph silently takes every
        Poetry controller down with it, so the registrar warns once per
        unregistered identifier in the browser console, and the
        <%= link_to "system-test helpers", testing_path(anchor: "testers") %>
        ship the same guard as an assertion. Your editor checks your
        controllers; <code>poetry:editor</code> lists Poetry's for the
        <%= link_to "Stimulus language server", editors_path(anchor: "herb") %>
        so a hand-written <code>poetry--core--dialog#open</code> is never
        flagged as unknown.
      </p>
    <% end %>
    <%= poetry_code_block(code: snippets[:tooling], language: "shell", label: "Tooling") %>
    <%= poetry_code_block(code: snippets[:guard], language: "ruby", label: "System test") %>
  </section>
</div>
